Since early March 2026, commercial traffic through the Strait of Hormuz has been severely disrupted. The Joint War Committee of the London insurance market lists the Gulf, the Strait and the Gulf of Oman as a listed area, so every call needs additional war-risk cover, and many owners will not send ships in at all. Ships that do trade the area face escort or routing instructions, long waits at anchor and a real risk of attack or mines.

This brief does not forecast when the Strait reopens. It sets out what changes in a tanker or LPG fixture while the disruption lasts, and the points we agree with both sides before a ship is fixed. The situation changes daily: treat it as a checklist, not legal advice.

## What the disruption has done to the market

- Middle East Gulf loadings of crude, clean products and LPG are sharply lower, and buyers in Asia and India have turned to Atlantic-basin supply. Longer voyages tie up more ships for the same cargo, which supports tanker and VLGC freight.
- US Gulf LPG exports reached record levels in spring 2026 as Asian buyers replaced Gulf cargoes, and US Gulf–Asia VLGC freight (the BLPG3 lane) set new highs in May.
- Ships that would normally ballast back to the Gulf are repositioning to the US Gulf, West Africa and Brazil instead, so the Panama-or-Cape and ballast-leg decisions matter more than ever.
- Bypass routes have gained weight: Red Sea loadings fed by Saudi Arabia's East–West pipeline, Fujairah outside the Strait, and Iraqi and Kurdish crude via the pipeline to Ceyhan on Türkiye's Mediterranean coast.
- Red Sea and Bab el-Mandeb risk remains elevated, so Suez routings for these cargoes need their own war-risk review.

## Checklist before you fix

1. War clause: check which clause applies. BIMCO VOYWAR (voyage charters) and CONWARTIME (time charters) let owners refuse to enter areas that become dangerous and pass additional war-risk costs to charterers. Many charterers' forms amend these clauses, so read the version in your charter party.
1. Additional premium: agree who pays the additional war-risk premium (AP), any kidnap and ransom cover and crew war bonuses, whether they are capped, and when the premium is charged (per entry, per call, per period).
1. Laycan and notice of readiness: if ships wait for escorts or clearance before entering, agree whether that time counts as laytime or is treated as a deviation. Write it down; do not rely on general exceptions.
1. Alternative ports: name the substitute load or discharge options and the freight difference in advance, so the ship can be redirected without a new negotiation.
1. Sanctions and AIS: ships that turn off AIS or carry out unplanned ship-to-ship transfers in the region are compliance red flags. We ask for voyage history and documents before an offer goes to the charterer.
1. Insurance: confirm that hull and machinery, P&I and war-risk underwriters have accepted the voyage, and that the cover matches the route actually being sailed.

## LPG and ammonia: specific points

Ras Tanura, Ju'aymah, Ruwais, Mina al-Ahmadi and the Qatari terminals normally supply a large share of the world's seaborne LPG, and the Gulf is also a major ammonia export region. With those cargoes constrained, VLGC demand has shifted to the US Gulf and other Atlantic exporters, and MGC ammonia buyers have been looking for replacement sources. For a VLGC voyage to Asia we now price Panama, the Cape and, where relevant, Suez side by side, with war-risk and waiting costs as separate lines.

## How we quote while the disruption lasts

Every freight idea we send shows the base freight, the assumed waiting days and the war-risk costs on separate lines, together with the clause that allocates them. Charterers can then compare an offer that includes the risk with one that leaves it open, instead of finding out after loading.

